review.raw:
VERDICT
Correct: your code correctly generates the FizzBuzz sequence up to n and places every element in the right spot.

REVIEW MY CODE

What you did well:

You pre-allocated the vector size with vector ans(n);, which avoids costly dynamic resizing during the loop.

You used direct indexing ans[i-1], which writes elements directly into their designated positions.

You correctly ordered your checks so that the case where both 3 and 5 divide the number is handled before checking 3 or 5 individually.

Is it correct:

Yes, it is fully correct and passes all test cases.

Logical mistakes and edge cases:

No logic bugs, but there is redundant branching: the outer condition if (i%3 == 0 || i%5 == 0) repeats the modulo checks that you immediately do again inside the nested if-else chain.

Edge cases handled properly: n = 1 (returns ["1"]), multiples of only 3 (returns "Fizz"), multiples of only 5 (returns "Buzz"), and multiples of 15 (returns "FizzBuzz").

Time and space complexity:

Time complexity: O(n) because the loop runs exactly n times, doing constant work at each step.

Space complexity: O(1) auxiliary space (or O(n) total space including the return vector) because no extra data structures are used aside from the required output.

Why your approach works:

For each number from 1 to n, it tests divisibility by 3 and 5 using the remainder operator %. Based on the result, it writes the appropriate string into the list.

ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED

Approach 1: Naive If-Else Chain

Where it comes from:

Canonical beginner solution found in classic textbooks and LeetCode editorial Approach 1.

Idea:

For every number from 1 to n, check the most restrictive condition first (both 3 and 5 divide i, meaning i % 15 == 0). If false, check divisible by 3, then divisible by 5, and if none match, convert the number to text.

C++ Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    vector<string> fizzBuzz(int n) {
        vector<string> ans;
        ans.reserve(n);
        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            if (i % 15 == 0) {
                ans.push_back("FizzBuzz");
            } else if (i % 3 == 0) {
                ans.push_back("Fizz");
            } else if (i % 5 == 0) {
                ans.push_back("Buzz");
            } else {
                ans.push_back(to_string(i));
            }
        }
        return ans;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n) because it checks each number from 1 to n once.

Space Complexity:

O(1) extra space beyond the output array.

What makes the next approach better:

Approach 1 hardcodes every combination. If you add more rules (like 7 for "Jazz"), the number of combinations multiplies exponentially. String concatenation eliminates combinatorial explosion.

Approach 2: String Concatenation

Where it comes from:

LeetCode editorial Approach 2.

Idea:

Start with an empty string for each number. If divisible by 3, attach "Fizz". If divisible by 5, attach "Buzz". If the string remains empty, convert the number to text.

C++ Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    vector<string> fizzBuzz(int n) {
        vector<string> ans(n);
        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            string s = "";
            if (i % 3 == 0) s += "Fizz";
            if (i % 5 == 0) s += "Buzz";
            if (s.empty()) s = to_string(i);
            ans[i - 1] = s;
        }
        return ans;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n) because string append of small strings runs in O(1) time per number.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space beyond the output list.

What makes the next approach better:

While concatenation solves the combination explosion, the divisors (3 and 5) and words ("Fizz" and "Buzz") are still hardcoded into multiple if conditions. A hash map or list of pairs makes it completely dynamic and extensible.

Approach 3: Hash Map / Rule Mapping

Where it comes from:

LeetCode editorial Approach 3, often requested in software engineering interviews to test Open-Closed Principle.

Idea:

Store divisor-word pairs in an ordered list or map. For each number, iterate through the mappings, appending the word whenever the number is divisible by that mapping's key.

C++ Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    vector<string> fizzBuzz(int n) {
        vector<string> ans(n);
        vector<pair<int, string>> rules = {
            {3, "Fizz"},
            {5, "Buzz"}
        };
        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            string s = "";
            for (const auto& rule : rules) {
                if (i % rule.first == 0) {
                    s += rule.second;
                }
            }
            if (s.empty()) {
                s = to_string(i);
            }
            ans[i - 1] = s;
        }
        return ans;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(m * n) where m is the number of mapping rules (m = 2 here, so O(n)).

Space Complexity:

O(m) space to store the mapping rules, which is O(1) for fixed rules.

What makes the next approach better:

Modulo (%) operations require hardware division, which is noticeably slower than integer addition. We can avoid modulo altogether by using counters.

Approach 4: Modulo-Free Two Counters

Where it comes from:

Advanced low-level optimization discussions on LeetCode and competitive programming blogs.

Idea:

Instead of computing i % 3 and i % 5, maintain two separate counters: one that counts up to 3, and one that counts up to 5. When a counter reaches its limit, reset it to 0 and trigger the word.

C++ Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    vector<string> fizzBuzz(int n) {
        vector<string> ans(n);
        int c3 = 0, c5 = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            c3++;
            c5++;
            if (c3 == 3 && c5 == 5) {
                ans[i - 1] = "FizzBuzz";
                c3 = 0;
                c5 = 0;
            } else if (c3 == 3) {
                ans[i - 1] = "Fizz";
                c3 = 0;
            } else if (c5 == 5) {
                ans[i - 1] = "Buzz";
                c5 = 0;
            } else {
                ans[i - 1] = to_string(i);
            }
        }
        return ans;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n) doing purely simple additions, comparisons, and resets without division.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space using two integer counters.

WHERE I STAND

Which approach you used:

You used a variation of Approach 1 (Naive If-Else Chain) with an extra outer wrapper condition.

Which approach does an interviewer expect:

Interviewers use FizzBuzz to check basic code fluency, but they almost always test for code extensibility. They expect you to start with Approach 1 or 2, and then pivot to Approach 3 (Rule Mapping) when they ask follow-ups.

Common follow-up questions and how to answer them:

Question 1: What if we add more rules, like 7 -> "Jazz" and 11 -> "Dazz"?

Answer: Explain that an if-else chain blows up because every combination (3x5, 3x7, 5x7, 3x5x7, etc.) needs its own branch. Switch to Approach 3 with a list of pairs so any new rule is added with just one line.

Question 2: Modulo % operations are slow in hardware. Can you do this without %?

Answer: Use Approach 4 with rolling counters that reset whenever they hit 3 or 5.

Question 3: What if n is very large, say 1 billion, and we only need to print?

Answer: Avoid generating strings or storing an array. Directly print to standard output using buffered I/O, or use multiple worker threads splitting the range into chunks.

WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER

The key insight:

Check divisibility by building strings piece by piece, or map rules to containers, so you never have to hardcode combinations.

Checklist for next time:

Avoid redundant checks: if you check (A || B) in the outer if, you do not need to recheck both inside.

Think about extensibility early: if a problem hints at multiple conditions combining together, prefer concatenation over nested branching.

Pre-allocate output containers with ans.reserve(n) or vector ans(n) for cleaner, faster memory usage.

The pattern:

Simulation and Rule-based String Construction.

